WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.206
A practical SOC 2 readiness routine begins with commitments and scope.

2
00:00:05.206 --> 00:00:12.778
Define the service organization, the service or system being reported on,
customer commitments, relevant environments, dependencies,

3
00:00:12.778 --> 00:00:16.564
and selected Trust Services Categories. Security is common.

4
00:00:16.564 --> 00:00:22.243
Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, and Privacy depend on
service needs and promises.

5
00:00:22.243 --> 00:00:28.396
Good scope connects what customers rely on to the controls the
organization operates.

6
00:00:28.396 --> 00:00:32.182
Next, map controls to criteria, risks, and commitments.

7
00:00:32.182 --> 00:00:37.388
A control should not exist only because it sounds audit friendly.

8
00:00:37.388 --> 00:00:42.594
It should support a defined objective. Access reviews support authorized
access.

9
00:00:42.594 --> 00:00:47.799
Change management supports controlled system change. Monitoring supports
detection and response.

10
00:00:47.799 --> 00:00:55.372
Vendor reviews support third-party risk management. Backup tests support
recoverability. Policies support consistent expectations. Assign owners.

11
00:00:55.372 --> 00:01:03.890
Every control needs someone who understands what must happen, how often it
must happen, what evidence is required,

12
00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:08.150
where that evidence lives, and how exceptions are handled.

13
00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:12.882
Control ownership should be cross-functional where the work is cross-
functional.

14
00:01:12.882 --> 00:01:20.928
Engineering, security, IT, HR, operations, and vendor management may all
contribute to the same SOC 2 program.

15
00:01:20.928 --> 00:01:23.294
Choose the report path honestly.

16
00:01:23.294 --> 00:01:29.920
Type I focuses on whether controls are suitably designed at a point in
time.

17
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:37.019
It can be useful for early readiness and customer assurance when the
program is new.

18
00:01:37.019 --> 00:01:42.698
Type II focuses on design and operating effectiveness over a defined
period.

19
00:01:42.698 --> 00:01:50.743
It usually requires stronger recurring evidence because the organization
must show that controls operated consistently over time.

20
00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:52.543
Prepare evidence workflows.

21
00:01:52.543 --> 00:01:59.642
Use ticketing, access review systems, monitoring tools, incident response
records, vendor review processes, backup tests,

22
00:01:59.642 --> 00:02:04.375
and change management workflows to create evidence as work happens.

23
00:02:04.375 --> 00:02:10.054
Do not wait until the review period ends to discover missing records.

24
00:02:10.054 --> 00:02:16.206
Evidence should be accurate, timely, traceable, and honest. Track
exceptions and remediate gaps.

25
00:02:16.206 --> 00:02:20.466
A sustainable program does not pretend everything is perfect.

26
00:02:20.466 --> 00:02:25.672
It identifies issues, evaluates impact, assigns remediation, follows up,
and improves.

27
00:02:25.672 --> 00:02:30.404
Communicate honestly with auditors and stakeholders at the appropriate
level.

28
00:02:30.404 --> 00:02:34.190
Do not fabricate evidence or misrepresent control operation.

29
00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:40.816
The core message is simple: Type I proves design at a point in time;

30
00:02:40.816 --> 00:02:43.656
Type II demonstrates operation over time.

31
00:02:43.656 --> 00:02:47.442
Both depend on scope, controls, owners, and evidence.

32
00:02:47.442 --> 00:02:54.160
The strongest SOC 2 programs treat readiness as normal operations, not a
last-minute audit event.
